<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mkwawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1855–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mkwawa was chief of the Hehe in the southern highlands of present-day Tanzania from about 1880 to 1898. He made the Hehe the dominant power of the region and destroyed a German column at Lugalo in 1891. After the Germans stormed his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalenga">Kalenga</a> in 1894 he fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war until 1898, when, cornered, he shot himself; his skull was returned to Tanzania in 1954.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mkwawa was born in Luhota, the son and successor of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munyigumba">Munyigumba</a>. His full name was Mkwavinyika Munyigumba Mwamuyinga, and the short form Mkwawa comes from Mukwava, itself a shortening of Mukwavinyika, meaning conqueror of many lands. As a small child he was called Ndesalasi, troublemaker, and as an adult he bore a long praise name describing a leader who takes control of the forests, is aggressive to men and polite to women, and whom only death can take away. His family descended from an immigrant from the north named Mufwimi, a name meaning a hunter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalenga">Kalenga</a> in the Iringa region, Mkwawa opposed German colonisation. In July 1891 the German commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emil-von-zelewski">Emil von Zelewski</a> led a battalion of 320 askaris, with officers and porters, to subdue the Hehe. On 17 August Mkwawa's army of 3,000, armed with spears and only a few guns, attacked them at Lugalo, quickly overpowered the column and killed Zelewski.</p><p>On 28 October 1894 the Germans under the new commissioner, Colonel Freiherr Friedrich von Schele, attacked the fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalenga">Kalenga</a>. They took it, but Mkwawa escaped and waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign against them for almost four years. His brother Chambila, whose name roughly means hero, fought beside him to the end. On 19 July 1898, surrounded at the Mlambalasi Rock Shelter, Mkwawa shot himself to avoid capture, and his body was buried a few metres from the shelter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>German soldiers cut off his head, and the skull was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and probably ended up in the Übersee-Museum Bremen. In 1918 Horace Byatt, the British administrator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>, proposed demanding its return, both to reward the Hehe for their help in the war and as a sign that German power had ended, and Article 246 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1919 required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to hand it over. The Germans denied that it had been taken, and the British government held that it could not be traced.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, Sir Edward Twining, Governor of Tanganyika, took up the matter and visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> museum himself in 1953. Of its 2000 skulls, 84 came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>; from those whose measurements resembled living relatives of Mkwawa he chose the only one with a bullet hole. It was returned on 9 July 1954 and is kept in the Mkwawa Memorial Museum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalenga">Kalenga</a>, near Iringa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chief Mkwawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hehe under Chief <b>Mkwawa</b> resisted from 1891 to 1894 and were defeated because rival peoples supported the Germans; after years of guerrilla war Mkwawa, cornered, killed himself in 1898.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1879 his son <b>Mkwawa</b> won a succession struggle and built the Hehe into the dominant power of the area.</blockquote><a href="/state/hehe-kingdom">Hehe Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
